\begin{abstract}
We compare multinomial logistic regression (LR) with $k$-nearest neighbours (kNN, $k$ chosen by inner cross-validation) on Iris (OpenML dataset 61) by 10 repetitions of stratified 5-fold cross-validation.
LR reaches a mean accuracy of \LRmean\% (95\% interval \LRlo--\LRhi\%), kNN \KNNmean\% (\KNNlo--\KNNhi\%); the paired difference of \Dmean{} percentage points (pp; \Dlo{} to \Dhi) does not resolve a difference.
Runs on an Ibex CPU node (\texttt{\IbexNode}) and locally gave \Identical{} numbers.
\end{abstract}

\section{Introduction}
Iris \citep{fisher1936} is small and nearly separable. We ask whether paired, repeated cross-validation shows multinomial LR and tuned kNN to differ on it, by how much, and whether the computation is reproducible.

\section{Data and methods}
\paragraph{Data.} The code downloads OpenML \citep{vanschoren2014openml} dataset 61 (version \DataVersion, MD5 checked) and asserts 150 instances, 4 features, 3 classes of 50.

\paragraph{Classifiers.} Both standardise the features in a pipeline fitted on training data only (scikit-learn \citep{pedregosa2011}).
LR uses the multinomial (softmax) loss with an L2 penalty, $C=1$, the L-BFGS solver and at most \MaxIter{} iterations (\ConvWarn{} convergence warnings); it is not tuned.
kNN (Euclidean distance, uniform weights) chooses $k\in\Kgrid$ by stratified 5-fold cross-validation on each outer training set only, takes the smallest $k$ on ties, and is refit on the outer training set.

\paragraph{Evaluation.} 10 repetitions of stratified 5-fold cross-validation (outer seed \OuterSeed) give 50 outer folds of 120 training and 30 test instances, the same for both methods, so each fold yields a paired accuracy difference.
Because the folds share training data, a $t$-interval treating the 50 fold values as independent is too narrow, so we use the corrected resampled $t$ interval \citep{nadeau2003}, $\bar{x}\pm t_{0.975,49}\sqrt{(1/50+30/120)\,s^2}$, where $s^2$ is the sample variance of the 50 fold values, for each method and for the paired differences.

\paragraph{Reproducibility.} The same script, data and seeds ran locally (\CPULocal{} CPU) and as SLURM job \IbexJob{} on Ibex node \texttt{\IbexNode} (\CPUIbex{} CPU), with the same software (\Versions) and single-threaded BLAS. The runs count as identical only if every fold accuracy, chosen $k$, prediction and summary value is exactly equal.

\section{Results}
Results are in Table~\ref{tab:results} and Figure~\ref{fig:acc}. LR is more accurate in \FoldsLR{} folds, kNN in \FoldsKNN, and they tie in \FoldsTied.
The difference of \Dmean{} pp has interval \Dlo{} to \Dhi{} pp ($p=\Dp$), so no difference is resolved; a naive $t$-interval ignoring the dependence between folds would give \Dnaivelo{} to \Dnaivehi{} pp and wrongly suggest an advantage for LR.
In \VariantsCoverZero{} sensitivity variants (other $k$ grids, no standardisation, tuned $C$ for LR, three other outer seeds) the interval also contains zero (mean differences \VariantDmin{} to \VariantDmax{} pp).
Inner cross-validation chose $k$ from \Kmin{} to \Kmax{} (median \Kmedian), never at the grid edge.

